# Architecture Rules

## Package-by-Feature
Each feature is its own package under `com.example.blog.<feature>`. Cross-cutting concerns live in `shared/`. There is **no** `controller/`, `service/`, `repository/` top-level package — that's package-by-layer and we don't do it here.

## Layer Isolation
`@RestController` handles HTTP only: parse, delegate, return. Any `if` in a controller that is not about HTTP belongs in the service.

## Domain Repository Interface + JPA Impl
Services depend on a **package-private domain interface** (`PostRepository`), not on `JpaRepository` directly. The Spring Data implementation (`JpaPostRepository extends JpaRepository<Post, UUID>` and `implements PostRepository`) lives in the same package and is also package-private.

Why: keeps the service layer ignorant of Spring Data, makes it trivial to swap to an `InMemoryPostRepository` for fast tests, and prevents callers from reaching for `findAll(Pageable)` and other JPA-isms that bypass the domain contract.

```java
// PostRepository.java (package-private)
interface PostRepository {
    List<Post> findAll();
    Optional<Post> findById(UUID id);
    Post save(Post post);
    boolean deleteById(UUID id);
}

// JpaPostRepository.java (package-private)
interface JpaPostRepository extends JpaRepository<Post, UUID>, PostRepository {}
```

## DTOs are Records; Entities are Classes
DTOs (the public API contract) are Java records — immutable, validated at the boundary. JPA entities are **mutable classes** with `@Id`, `@Entity`, and a protected no-arg constructor. JPA cannot manage records.

The mapper (`@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")`) is the only place that crosses the line. Entities never leave the service layer.

## Service Owns Transactions
`@Transactional` on `@Service` methods. `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` on read paths. Never annotate controllers or repositories.

## Validation at Entry
`@Valid @RequestBody` on every write endpoint. Constraints (`@NotBlank`, `@Size`, etc.) live on the DTO fields, never on the entity.

## Global Exception Handling
One `@ControllerAdvice` (`shared/GlobalExceptionHandler`) translates domain exceptions and validation failures into the project's `ErrorResponse` envelope. Do not catch-and-translate inside controllers.

```java
public record ErrorResponse(Instant timestamp, int status, String error, String message) {}
```

`NotFoundException` (in `shared/`) is the canonical 404 trigger.

## Constructor Injection Only
`@RequiredArgsConstructor` on every Spring bean with collaborators. **Field injection (`@Autowired` on a field) is forbidden.**

## ID Strategy
UUID primary keys generated by the service (`UUID.randomUUID()`), not by the database. Keeps tests deterministic and avoids round-trip fetches after insert.
